LinkedIn News Asia shared a post: What=E2=80=99s the hardest part of starti=
ng out as a woman founder?

The share of women among founders in Singapore has grown over the last deca=
de, but progress has been slow. New research from LinkedIn's Economic Graph=
 shows that women made up 26.1% of all founders in 2015, rising to 31.8% in=
 2025 =E2=80=93 a 5.7 percentage point increase overall.

Women founders are best represented in industries like retail (51%), hospit=
als and health care (47.6%) and consumer services (42.3%). However, they ho=
ld a smaller share of founder roles in sectors like financial services (19.=
6%), technology, information and media (24.3%) and transportation and logis=
tics (19.4%).

Melly Ling, co=E2=80=91founder and CEO of Mole, says, =E2=80=9CIf you=E2=80=
=99re a woman thinking about starting a venture, I=E2=80=99ll be honest =E2=
=80=93 people will have opinions. They=E2=80=99ll say you=E2=80=99re either=
 too old, inexperienced, too early or too late. But what matters most is wh=
ether you=E2=80=99re clear about the problem you=E2=80=99re solving.=E2=80=
=9D

That judgement can come with added emotional pressure, says Christa Sabatha=
ly, founder of SPUN and an ex-Google country lead for new business partners=
hip. She says moving from a corporate career into entrepreneurship meant co=
nstant uncertainty while also becoming =E2=80=9Cthe emotional shock absorbe=
r so everyone else in the company could feel stable=E2=80=9D. The experienc=
e forced her to continuously outgrow the version of leadership that once ma=
de her successful.

What=E2=80=99s been the biggest challenge in your founder journey and what =
support helped you most in the early years? Have your say in our poll and s=
hare your experiences in the comments. #IWD26

